<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-second-derivative-test-and-saddle-points">
	<title>Hessians and the second derivative test</title>
	<introduction xml:id="intro-u5-hessian-second-derivative-test">
		<p>In <xref ref="sec-u3-critical-points-gradient-descent" />, critical points are found by solving
      <me>\nabla f(\mathbf a)=\mathbf 0.</me>
      This condition removes the linear term from the local approximation, but it does not determine whether <m>\mathbf a</m> is a local minimum, a local maximum, or a saddle point. The next term is quadratic. In <xref ref="subsec-u5-quadratic-forms-definiteness" />, eigenvalues determined the sign of a quadratic form. We now apply that linear algebra to the Hessian.</p>
	</introduction>
	<subsection xml:id="subsec-u5-local-behavior-quadratic-approximation">
		<title>Local behavior and quadratic approximation</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>A critical point need not be a local extremum. We first name the third possibility, then review the one-variable Taylor polynomial before writing its multivariable analogue.</p>
		</introduction>
		<definition>
			<statement>
				<p>
					A critical point of a function which is not a local minimum or local maximum is called a <term>saddle point</term>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<figure>
			<caption>A saddle point at the origin of the function <m>f(x,y) = y^2 - x^2</m>. Note that along the <m>x</m>-axis (<m>y = 0</m>), the function has a global maximum at <m>x = 0</m>, while along the <m>y</m>-axis (<m>x = 0</m>), the function has a global minimum at <m>y = 0</m>. This combination of maximum in one direction and minimum in another is characteristic of saddle points. Figure 4.48 from Edwin <q>Jed</q> Herman and Gilbert Strang, <em>Calculus Volume 3</em>, OpenStax, © 2018 Rice University, licensed under <url href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/" visual="CC BY-NC-SA 4.0" />; source: <url href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-7-maxima-minima-problems#CNX_Calc_Figure_14_07_002" visual="OpenStax Figure 4.48" />.</caption>
			<image source="calc3-fig-4-48.png" width="50%">
				<shortdescription>
					A saddle surface with the origin as the saddle point.
				</shortdescription>
				<description>
					<p>
						The 3D graph of <m>z=y^2-x^2</m> is shown with the saddle point at the origin. One cross-section curves upward like a valley, while the perpendicular cross-section curves downward like a ridge. The surface is colored blue on one side and red on the other, making the opposing curvatures visible.
					</p>
				</description>
			</image>
		</figure>
		<p xml:id="sec-10-1-second-derivtive-test">The second-order approximation explains this local behavior. We begin with the one-variable case.</p>
		<p>
			For a review, see <url href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/6-3-taylor-and-maclaurin-series">Section 6.3: Taylor and Maclaurin Series</url> in Strang and Herman's <em>Calculus Volume 2</em>.
		</p>
		<p>
			Recall that if a single-variable function <m>f</m> has <m>n</m> derivatives at <m>x = a</m>, then the <m>n</m>th Taylor polynomial for <m>f</m> at <m>a</m> is the function
			<md>
				<mrow>
					p_n(x) \amp = f(a) + f'(a)(x - a) + \frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x - a)^2 + \cdots + \frac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!}(x - a)^n
				</mrow>
				<mrow>
					\amp = \sum_{k=0}^n \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x - a)^k
				</mrow>
			</md>.
			In particular, for <m>n = 1</m> we obtain the <em>linear approximation</em>
			<me>
				p_1(x) = f(a) + f'(a) (x - a)
			</me>
			of <m>f</m>, and for <m>n = 2</m> we obtain the <em>quadratic approximation</em>
			<me>
				p_2(x) = f(a) + f'(a) (x - a) + f''(a) \frac{(x-a)^2}{2}
			</me>.
			The Taylor polynomial <m>p_n(x)</m> approximates the function <m>f(x)</m> near <m>x = a</m>, with the approximation improving as <m>n</m> increases.
		</p>
		<activity>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider the function <m>f(x) = \sqrt[3]{x}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Find the first and second Taylor polynomials for <m>f</m> at <m>x = 8</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					For <m>f(x) = \sqrt[3]{x}</m>, the values of the function and its first two derivatives at <m>x = 8</m> are as follows:
					<md>
						<mrow>
							f(x) \amp = \sqrt[3]{x} \amp f(8) \amp = 2
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							f'(x) \amp = \frac{1}{3x^{2/3}} \amp f'(8) \amp = \frac{1}{12}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							f''(x) \amp = \frac{-2}{9x^{5/3}} \amp f''(8) \amp = -\frac{1}{144}
						</mrow>
					</md>

					Thus, the first and second Taylor polynomials at <m>x = 8</m> are given by:
					<md>
						<mrow>
							p_1(x) \amp = f(8) + f'(8)(x - 8)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = 2 + \frac{1}{12}(x - 8)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							p_2(x) \amp = f(8) + f'(8)(x - 8) + \frac{f''(8)}{2!}(x - 8)^2
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = 2 + \frac{1}{12}(x - 8) - \frac{1}{288}(x - 8)^2
						</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
		<activity>
			<title>Using Taylor polynomials</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use these two polynomials to estimate <m>\sqrt[3]{11}</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Using the first Taylor polynomial at <m>x = 8</m>, we can estimate:
					<md>
						<mrow>
							\sqrt[3]{11} \approx p_1(11) \amp = 2 + \frac{1}{12}(11 - 8)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = 2.25
						</mrow>
					</md>

					Using the second Taylor polynomial at <m>x = 8</m>, we obtain:
					<md>
						<mrow>
							\sqrt[3]{11} \approx p_2(11) \amp = 2 + \frac{1}{12}(11 - 8) - \frac{1}{288}(11 - 8)^2
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = 2 + \frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{32}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = 2.21875
						</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
		<p>
			If <m>a</m> is a critical point of <m>f</m>, then the quadratic approximation of <m>f</m> is given by the expression
			<me>
				f(a) + f''(a) \frac{(x-a)^2}{2}
			</me>
			If <m>f''(a) \gt 0</m>, then the quadratic approximation of <m>f</m> is an upward pointing parabola with a vertex at <m>(a,f(a))</m>. This approximation has a minimum at this vertex, explaining <m>a</m> is a local minimum of the function <m>f</m> (since <m>f</m> is closely approximated by its quadratic approximation). Similarly, if <m>f''(a) \lt 0</m>, then the quadratic approximation is a downward pointing parabola with a maximum at <m>(a,f(a))</m>, explaining why <m>a</m> is a local maximum of the function <m>f</m>.
		</p>
		<p>In several variables, the <xref ref="sec-8-5-gradient" text="custom">gradient</xref> replaces the first derivative and the <xref ref="def-8-5-hessian" text="custom">Hessian</xref> replaces the second derivative.</p>
		<definition xml:id="def-u5-quadratic-approximation">
			<title>Quadratic approximation</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The <term>quadratic approximation</term>
					<m>Q(\mathbf{x})</m> of a scalar-valued function <m>f</m> near a point <m>\mathbf{a}</m> is the function defined by
					<me>
						Q(\mathbf{x})
						=
						f(\mathbf{a})
						+
						\nabla f(\mathbf{a})\cdot(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{a})
						+
						\frac12(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{a})^T H_f(\mathbf{a})(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{a})
					</me>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<remark>
			<p>
				The quadratic approximation of a function <m>f</m> refines the linear approximation via the gradient that we previously encountered. In particular, if the second derivatives of <m>f</m> are continuous, then
				<me>
					\lim_{\mathbf{x} \to \mathbf{a}} \frac{f(\mathbf{x}) - Q(\mathbf{x})}{\| \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{a} \|^2} = 0
				</me>.
				If <m>\mathbf{x}</m> is close to <m>\mathbf{a}</m>, then <m>\| \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{a} \|^2</m> is much smaller than <m>\| \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{a} \|</m>, so that the quadratic approximation <m>Q(\mathbf{x})</m> to <m>f</m> is in general a much better approximation than the linear approximation <m>L(\mathbf{x})</m>.
			</p>
		</remark>
		<activity>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Compute the gradient and the Hessian of the function <m>f(x, y) = \ln(xy - 1)</m> at the point <m>(x, y) = (1, 2)</m>, use these values to determine the quadratic approximation to <m>f</m> at the point <m>(1,2)</m>, and then use the quadratic approximation to estimate the value of <m>f</m> at <m>(1.2, 1.8)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We calculate that
					<me>
						f_x = \frac{y}{xy - 1} \quad\text{and}\quad f_y = \frac{x}{xy - 1}
					</me>.
					So <m>(\nabla f)(1,2) = (2,1)</m>. Next, we compute that
					<md>
						<mrow>
							f_{xx} \amp = \frac{-y^2}{(xy - 1)^2} \amp
							f_{yy} \amp = \frac{-x^2}{(xy - 1)^2} \amp
							f_{xy} \amp = f_{yx} = \frac{-1}{(xy - 1)^2}
						</mrow>
					</md>.
					Evaluating at <m>(1, 2)</m>, we find that
					<me>
						H_f(1, 2) = \begin{bmatrix}
						\frac{-4}{1} \amp \frac{-1}{1} \\
						\frac{-1}{1} \amp \frac{-1}{1}
						\end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}
						-4 \amp -1 \\
						-1 \amp -1
						\end{bmatrix}
					</me>.
					Since <me>f(1,2) = \ln(1) = 0</me>, using the Hessian and gradient, we find that the quadratic approximation of <m>f</m> near <m>(1,2)</m> is given by
					<md>
						<mrow>
							Q(x,y) \amp =
							(2,1) \cdot ( x - 1, y - 2 ) + \frac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} x - 1 \amp y - 2 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix}
							-4 \amp -1 \\
							-1 \amp -1
							\end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x - 1 \\ y - 2 \end{bmatrix}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = 2(x - 1) + (y-2) - 2 (x - 1)^2 - (x-1)(y-2) - (1/2) (y-2)^2
						</mrow>
					</md>.
					So
					<md>
						<mrow>
							Q(1.2,1.8) \amp = 2(0.2) + (-0.2) - 2 (0.2)^2 - (0.2)(-0.2) - (1/2) (-0.2)^2
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = 0.4 - 0.2 - 0.08 + 0.04 - 0.02
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = 0.14
						</mrow>
					</md>.
					The actual value is <m>f(1.2,1.8)\approx0.1484</m>, while the quadratic approximation is <m>0.14</m>. Thus the quadratic approximation is quite accurate. For comparison, the linear approximation <m>L</m> at <m>(1,2)</m> is given by
					<me>
						L(x,y) =2(0.2) + (-0.2) = 0.2
					</me>,
					which is far less accurate.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
		<activity>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>f(x, y) = \cos(x/y)</m>. Use the gradient and Hessian to obtain a quadratic approximation of the function <m>f(x, y)</m> near <m>\mathbf{a} = (0, 1)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We calculate that
					<me>
						\nabla f(x,y) = \left( - \frac{\sin(x/y)}{y}, \frac{x \sin(x/y)}{y^2} \right)
					</me>
					In particular, <m>\nabla f(0,1) = (0,0)</m>. Next, we calculate that
					<md>
						<mrow>
							\amp f_{xx} = -\frac{1}{y^2}\cos\left(\frac{x}{y}\right), \quad   f_{yy} = \frac{x}{y^4}\left(2y\sin\left(\frac{x}{y}\right) + x\cos\left(\frac{x}{y}\right)\right)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp f_{xy} = f_{yx} = \frac{1}{y^3}\left(y\sin\left(\frac{x}{y}\right) + x\cos\left(\frac{x}{y}\right)\right)
						</mrow>
					</md>
					So
					<me>
						H_f(0,1) = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
					</me>
					Since <m>f(0,1) = \cos(0) = 1</m>, the quadratic approximation of <m>f</m> at <m>(0,1)</m> is thus given by
					<me>
						Q(x,y) = 1 - x^2/2
					</me>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
	</subsection>
	<subsection xml:id="sec-general-second-derivative-test">
		<title>Hessian eigenvalues and the general second derivative test</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>Suppose <m>\mathbf a</m> is a <xref ref="def-u3-critical-point" text="custom">critical point</xref>. Then
      <me>\nabla f(\mathbf a)=\mathbf 0,</me>
      so the <xref ref="def-u5-quadratic-approximation" text="custom">quadratic approximation</xref> becomes
      <me>f(\mathbf a+\mathbf h)\approx f(\mathbf a)+\frac12\mathbf h^T H_f(\mathbf a)\mathbf h.</me>
      Thus the local behavior is controlled by the <xref ref="def-u5-quadratic-form" text="custom">quadratic form</xref> associated with the Hessian. Because the Hessian is symmetric, <xref ref="thm-principal-axes" text="custom">the spectral theorem</xref> gives an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors. The signs of the corresponding eigenvalues determine the sign of the quadratic term.</p>
		</introduction>
		<theorem xml:id="thm-u5-general-second-derivative-test">
			<title>Second derivative test</title>
			<statement>
				<p>Let <m>f:\R^n\to\R</m> be twice continuously differentiable near a critical point <m>\mathbf a</m>, and let
      <me>H=H_f(\mathbf a).</me>
				</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>If every eigenvalue of <m>H</m> is strictly positive, then <m>\mathbf a</m> is a strict local minimum.</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>If every eigenvalue of <m>H</m> is strictly negative, then <m>\mathbf a</m> is a strict local maximum.</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>If <m>H</m> has at least one strictly positive eigenvalue and at least one strictly negative eigenvalue, then <m>\mathbf a</m> is a saddle point.</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
				<p>In all other cases, the test is inconclusive.</p>
			</statement>
			<proof>
				<p>Since <m>\mathbf a</m> is a critical point,
      <me>f(\mathbf a+\mathbf h)-f(\mathbf a)=\frac12\mathbf h^TH\mathbf h+r(\mathbf h),</me>
      where
      <me>\frac{r(\mathbf h)}{\|\mathbf h\|^2}\longrightarrow0\qquad\text{as }\mathbf h\longrightarrow\mathbf0.</me>
				</p>
				<p>Suppose first that every eigenvalue of <m>H</m> is positive, and let <m>\mu&gt;0</m> be the smallest eigenvalue. By <xref ref="thm-10-1-diagonalization-theorem" text="custom">the quadratic-form diagonalization theorem</xref>,
      <me>\mathbf h^TH\mathbf h\geq\mu\|\mathbf h\|^2.</me>
      For sufficiently small nonzero <m>\mathbf h</m>,
      <me>|r(\mathbf h)|\leq\frac{\mu}{4}\|\mathbf h\|^2.</me>
      Therefore
      <me>f(\mathbf a+\mathbf h)-f(\mathbf a)\geq\frac{\mu}{2}\|\mathbf h\|^2-\frac{\mu}{4}\|\mathbf h\|^2&gt;0.</me>
      Hence <m>\mathbf a</m> is a strict local minimum. The negative-eigenvalue case follows by applying the same argument to <m>-f</m>.</p>
				<p>Finally, suppose <m>H</m> has a positive eigenvalue <m>\lambda_+</m> with unit eigenvector <m>\mathbf v_+</m>, and a negative eigenvalue <m>\lambda_-</m> with unit eigenvector <m>\mathbf v_-</m>. Along the two lines through <m>\mathbf a</m>,
      <me>f(\mathbf a+t\mathbf v_+)-f(\mathbf a)=\frac12\lambda_+t^2+o(t^2)&gt;0</me>
      and
      <me>f(\mathbf a+t\mathbf v_-)-f(\mathbf a)=\frac12\lambda_-t^2+o(t^2)\lt0</me>
      for all sufficiently small nonzero <m>t</m>. Nearby values therefore occur on both sides of <m>f(\mathbf a)</m>, so <m>\mathbf a</m> is a saddle point.</p>
			</proof>
		</theorem>
		<warning>
			<p>A zero Hessian eigenvalue does not classify the critical point. It means the quadratic approximation has a flat direction, so higher-order terms may matter.</p>
		</warning>
		<activity xml:id="act-u5-predicting-from-hessian-eigenvalues">
			<title>Predicting from Hessian eigenvalues</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose <m>\mathbf{a}</m> is a critical point of a scalar-valued function <m>f</m>. For each list of eigenvalues of <m>H_f(\mathbf{a})</m>, classify the critical point as a local minimum, local maximum, saddle point, or inconclusive.
				</p>
				<ol marker="a">
					<li>
						<p>
							<m>3,5</m>
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<m>-2,-7</m>
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<m>4,-1</m>
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<m>0,2</m>
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
				<p>
					<em>Learning outcomes.</em> U5-LO5.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					If the eigenvalues are <m>3,5</m>, then the Hessian is positive definite, so the critical point is a local minimum.
				</p>
				<p>
					If the eigenvalues are <m>-2,-7</m>, then the Hessian is negative definite, so the critical point is a local maximum.
				</p>
				<p>
					If the eigenvalues are <m>4,-1</m>, then the Hessian is indefinite, so the critical point is a saddle point.
				</p>
				<p>
					If the eigenvalues are <m>0,2</m>, the test is inconclusive. The zero eigenvalue means the second-order approximation has a flat direction, so the second derivative test alone does not decide the local behavior.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
		<activity>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					Consider the function <m>f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 - xz</m>. Find all critical points of <m>f</m> and determine the behavior of <m>f</m> near each critical point.
				</p>
			</introduction>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						First find the critical points.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						We compute that <m>\nabla f(x,y,z) = ( 2x - z, 2y, -x )</m>, which vanishes only at <m>(0,0,0)</m>, which is the only critical point.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Now classify each critical point.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						The Hessian is the constant matrix
						<me>
							H_f = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ -1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
						</me>.
						The associated quadratic form is
						<md>
							<mrow>
								\mathbf{h}^T H_f\mathbf{h}
								\amp =
								2h_1^2+2h_2^2-2h_1h_3.
							</mrow>
						</md>
						This quadratic form takes both positive and negative values. For example,
						<me>
							\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}^T
							H_f
							\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}
							=2&gt;0,
						</me>
						but
						<me>
							\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\3\end{bmatrix}^T
							H_f
							\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\3\end{bmatrix}
							=
							2-6=-4\lt 0.
						</me>
						Thus <m>H_f</m> is indefinite. By the second derivative test, the critical point <m>(0,0,0)</m> is a saddle point.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</activity>
		<activity>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Classify the critical points of the function <m>f(x,y,z) = xy + yz + xz + xyz</m> using the second derivative test.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We compute that
					<me>
						\nabla f(x,y,z) =
						\begin{bmatrix}
						y + z + yz\\
						x + z + xz\\
						x + y + xy
						\end{bmatrix}
					</me>.
					Notice that <m>\nabla f(x,-1,z) = ( -1, x + z + xz, -1 )</m> is non-vanishing, so that there are no critical points on the plane <m>y = -1</m>. Now suppose that <m>\nabla f(x,y,z) = 0</m> and <m>y \neq -1</m>. The third entry tells us that <m>x = -y/(1 + y)</m>. Substituting this into the second equation gives that <m>-y/(1+y) + z - yz/(1+y) = 0</m>, i.e., that <m>z = y</m>. Substituting this into the first equation gives that <m>y^2 + 2y = 0</m>, i.e., that <m>y = 0</m> or <m>y = -2</m>. Substituting back gives the two critical points of <m>f</m>, namely <m>(0,0,0)</m> and <m>(-2,-2,-2)</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Next, we classify these critical points. We calculate that the Hessian of <m>f</m> is
					<me>
						H_f(x,y,z) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 + z \amp 1 + y \\ 1 + z \amp 0 \amp 1 + x \\ 1 + y \amp 1 + x \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
					</me>.
					In particular,
					<me>
						H_f(0,0,0) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
					</me>,
					which has characteristic polynomial
					<md>
						<mrow>
							\det \begin{bmatrix} \lambda \amp -1 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp \lambda \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp -1 \amp \lambda \end{bmatrix} \amp = \lambda( \lambda^2 - 1) - (-1) (-\lambda - 1) + (-1) (1 + \lambda)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \lambda^3 - 3\lambda - 2
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = (\lambda + 1)(\lambda^2 - \lambda - 2)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = (\lambda + 1)^2( \lambda - 2 )
						</mrow>
					</md>.
					So <m>H_f(0,0,0)</m> has eigenvalues <m>-1</m> and <m>2</m>, and so is indefinite, so that <m>f</m> has a saddle point at <m>(0,0,0)</m>. We have
					<me>
						H_f(-2,-2,-2) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
					</me>,
					which has characteristic polynomial
					<md>
						<mrow>
							\det \begin{bmatrix} \lambda \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp \lambda \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp \lambda \end{bmatrix} \amp = \lambda( \lambda^2 - 1) - (\lambda - 1) + (1 - \lambda)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \lambda^3 - 3 \lambda + 2
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = (\lambda - 1)(\lambda^2 + \lambda - 2)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = (\lambda - 1)^2(\lambda + 2)
						</mrow>
					</md>,
					so <m>H_f(-2,-2,-2)</m> has eigenvalues <m>1</m> and <m>-2</m>, and is thus an indefinite matrix. So <m>(-2,-2,-2)</m> is also a saddle point of <m>f</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
	</subsection>
	<subsection xml:id="sec-second-derivative-test-and-saddle-points-2">
		<title>The two-variable second derivative test</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>For a function of two variables, the Hessian at a critical point has the form
      <me>H_f(a,b)=\begin{bmatrix}f_{xx}(a,b)\amp f_{xy}(a,b)\\f_{xy}(a,b)\amp f_{yy}(a,b)\end{bmatrix}.</me>
      Its determinant is
      <me>D(a,b)=f_{xx}(a,b)f_{yy}(a,b)-f_{xy}(a,b)^2.</me>
      Since the determinant is the product of the two eigenvalues, <m>D</m> detects whether their signs agree or disagree. The standard two-variable test is therefore a shortcut for the general Hessian test.</p>
		</introduction>
		<activity xml:id="act-10-1-second-derivative-test-two-variables">
			<title>The <m>2\times2</m> definiteness criterion</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Show that a matrix
					<me>
						A = \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ b \amp d \end{bmatrix}
					</me>
					is positive definite if and only if <m>ad - b^2</m> is positive and <m>a &gt; 0</m>, negative definite if and only if <m>ad - b^2</m> is positive and <m>a \lt 0</m>, and indefinite if and only if <m>ad - b^2</m> is negative. It follows from this that the two-variable test is a special case of the general second derivative test.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>\lambda_1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m> be the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>. A direct calculation gives
					<md>
						<mrow>
							c_A(\lambda)
							\amp=\det(\lambda I_2-A)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp=(\lambda-a)(\lambda-d)-b^2
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp=\lambda^2-(a+d)\lambda+(ad-b^2).
						</mrow>
					</md>
					On the other hand, the characteristic polynomial is monic and has roots <m>\lambda_1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m>, so
					<md>
						<mrow>
							c_A(\lambda)
							\amp=(\lambda-\lambda_1)(\lambda-\lambda_2)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp=\lambda^2-(\lambda_1+\lambda_2)\lambda+\lambda_1\lambda_2.
						</mrow>
					</md>
					Comparing coefficients gives
					<me>
						a+d=\lambda_1+\lambda_2,
						\qquad
						ad-b^2=\lambda_1\lambda_2.
					</me>
				</p>
				<p>
					Suppose first that <m>A</m> is positive definite. Then <m>\lambda_1,\lambda_2&gt;0</m>, so <m>ad-b^2=\lambda_1\lambda_2&gt;0</m>. Also, by <xref ref="thm-10-1-positive-negative-definite-inequlaily" />,
					<me>
						a=\mathbf{e}_1^T A\mathbf{e}_1&gt;0.
					</me>
					Conversely, suppose <m>ad-b^2&gt;0</m> and <m>a&gt;0</m>. Then <m>ad&gt;b^2\geq 0</m>, so <m>d&gt;0</m> as well. Hence
					<me>
						\lambda_1+\lambda_2=a+d&gt;0,
						\qquad
						\lambda_1\lambda_2=ad-b^2&gt;0.
					</me>
					The positive product means that the two eigenvalues have the same sign, and the positive sum means that this sign is positive. Thus both eigenvalues are positive, so <m>A</m> is positive definite.
				</p>
				<p>
					The negative-definite case is analogous. If <m>A</m> is negative definite, then <m>\lambda_1,\lambda_2\lt 0</m>, so <m>ad-b^2&gt;0</m>, and <xref ref="thm-10-1-positive-negative-definite-inequlaily" /> gives <m>a=\mathbf{e}_1^T A\mathbf{e}_1\lt 0</m>. Conversely, if <m>ad-b^2&gt;0</m> and <m>a\lt 0</m>, then <m>d\lt 0</m>. Thus the eigenvalues have a positive product and a negative sum, so both eigenvalues are negative and <m>A</m> is negative definite.
				</p>
				<p>
					Finally, <m>A</m> is indefinite if and only if its two eigenvalues have opposite signs. This holds if and only if <m>\lambda_1\lambda_2\lt 0</m>, which, by the coefficient comparison above, holds if and only if <m>ad-b^2\lt 0</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
		<theorem xml:id="thm-u5-two-variable-second-derivative-test">
			<title>Two-variable second derivative test</title>
			<statement>
				<p>Let <m>f:\R^2\to\R</m> be twice continuously differentiable near a critical point <m>(a,b)</m>. Define
      <me>D(a,b)=f_{xx}(a,b)f_{yy}(a,b)-f_{xy}(a,b)^2.</me>
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<p>If <m>D(a,b)&gt;0</m> and <m>f_{xx}(a,b)&gt;0</m>, then <m>(a,b)</m> is a local minimum.</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>If <m>D(a,b)&gt;0</m> and <m>f_{xx}(a,b)\lt0</m>, then <m>(a,b)</m> is a local maximum.</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>If <m>D(a,b)\lt0</m>, then <m>(a,b)</m> is a saddle point.</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>If <m>D(a,b)=0</m>, the test is inconclusive.</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
			</statement>
			<proof>
				<p>Apply <xref ref="act-10-1-second-derivative-test-two-variables" /> to the Hessian matrix <m>H_f(a,b)</m>, then apply <xref ref="thm-u5-general-second-derivative-test" />.</p>
			</proof>
		</theorem>
		<activity>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use <xref ref="thm-u5-two-variable-second-derivative-test" text="custom">the two-variable second derivative test</xref> to determine if the critical point <m>(1,-2)</m> of <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 2x + 4y</m> found in <xref ref="act-u3-critical-point-quadratic-minimum" text="custom">the earlier activity</xref> is a local maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We apply the second derivative test. We compute the second-order partial derivatives of <m>f</m>. We calculate that
					<me>
						f_{xx}(x,y) = 2 \quad f_{yy} = 2 \quad\text{and} \quad f_{xy}(x,y) = 0
					</me>.
					So the discriminant is the constant function
					<me>D = f_{xx} f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = (2)(2) - (0)^2 = 4</me>.
					Since the discriminant is positive, by the second derivative test, the critical point <m>(1,-2)</m> is a local minimum.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
		<activity>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use <xref ref="thm-u5-two-variable-second-derivative-test" text="custom">the two-variable second derivative test</xref> to analyze the critical point <m>(0,0)</m> of <m>f(x,y) = x^2 - y^2</m> found in <xref ref="act-u3-critical-point-saddle-quadratic" text="custom">the earlier activity</xref>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We need to compute the second-order partial derivatives:
					<me>
						f_{xx} = 2 \quad f_{yy} = -2 \quad \text{and} \quad f_{xy} = 0
					</me>.
					So the discriminant is also the constant function
					<me>
						D = f_{xx} f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = (2)(-2) - (0)^2 = -4
					</me>.
					Since the discriminant is negative, the second derivative test tells us that <m>(0,0)</m> is a <em>saddle point</em> of <m>f</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
		<activity>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find all local maxima, local minima, and saddle points for the function
					<me>f(x,y) = x^2 + xy + y^2 - 6x + 6</me>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We begin by finding the critical points of <m>f</m>. We calculate that
					<me>
						f_x(x,y)=2x+y-6
						\qquad\text{and}\qquad
						f_y(x,y)=x+2y
					</me>.
					So the critical points satisfy
					<me>
						2x+y-6=0
						\qquad\text{and}\qquad
						x+2y=0.
					</me>
					The second equation gives <m>x=-2y</m>. Substituting into the first equation gives
					<me>
						2(-2y)+y-6=0,
					</me>
					so <m>-3y=6</m>, and therefore <m>y=-2</m>. Thus <m>x=4</m>, and the only critical point is <m>(4,-2)</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					To classify this critical point, we apply the second derivative test. We calculate that
					<me>
						f_{xx}(x,y)=2,\qquad f_{yy}(x,y)=2,\qquad f_{xy}(x,y)=1
					</me>.
					So the discriminant is the constant
					<me>
						D = f_{xx} f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = (2)(2) - (1)^2 = 3
					</me>.
					Since <m>D&gt;0</m> and <m>f_{xx}&gt;0</m>, the critical point <m>(4,-2)</m> is a local minimum.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
		<activity xml:id="act-u5-classify-gradient-descent-quadratic">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider the function
					<me>f(x,y)=x^2-3xy+3y^2+5y+2x.</me>
					Find all critical points of <m>f</m> and classify each as a local minimum, a local maximum, or a saddle point.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We find the critical points by solving <m>\nabla f(x,y)=\mathbf{0}</m>. Since
					<me>
						f_x(x,y)=2x-3y+2
						\qquad\text{and}\qquad
						f_y(x,y)=-3x+6y+5,
					</me>
					the critical points satisfy
					<me>
						2x-3y+2=0
						\qquad\text{and}\qquad
						-3x+6y+5=0.
					</me>
					The first equation gives <m>x=(3y-2)/2</m>. Substituting this expression into the second equation gives
					<md>
						<mrow>-3\left(\frac{3y-2}{2}\right)+6y+5 &amp;=0,</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{3y}{2} &amp;=-8,</mrow>
						<mrow>y &amp;=-\frac{16}{3}.</mrow>
					</md>
					It follows that
					<me>
						x=\frac{3(-16/3)-2}{2}=-9,
					</me>
					so the only critical point is <m>(-9,-16/3)</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					The Hessian matrix is the constant matrix
					<me>
						H_f(x,y)=
						\begin{bmatrix}
							2 &amp; -3 \\
							-3 &amp; 6
						\end{bmatrix}.
					</me>
					Thus the discriminant is
					<me>
						D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2=(2)(6)-(-3)^2=3&gt;0.
					</me>
					Since <m>D&gt;0</m> and <m>f_{xx}=2&gt;0</m>, the second derivative test tells us that <m>(-9,-16/3)</m> is a local minimum of <m>f</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
		<p>
			In the previous examples, the discriminant was constant because <m>f</m> was a quadratic function. Let us consider an example where the discriminant is nonconstant.
		</p>
		<activity>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find all local maxima, local minima, and saddle points for the function <m>f(x,y) = x^3 + y^2 + 2xy - 4x - 3y + 5</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We begin by finding the critical point. We calculate that
					<me>
						f_x(x,y) = 3x^2 + 2y - 4 \quad\text{and}\quad f_y(x,y) = 2y + 2x - 3
					</me>.
					Setting both equal to zero gives the pair of equations
					<me>
						3x^2 + 2y - 4 = 0 \quad\text{and}\quad 2y + 2x - 3 = 0
					</me>.
					We solve the second equation for <m>y</m>, i.e., that <m>y = 3/2 - x</m>, and then substitute this into the first equation, which leads to the equation <me>3x^2 + 2(3/2 - x) - 4 = 0</me>, which simplifies to the quadratic equation
					<me>
						3x^2 - 2x - 1 = 0
					</me>.
					The quadratic equation tells us that the roots of this equation are given by the formula
					<me>
						x = \frac{-(-2) \pm \sqrt{(-2)^2 - 4 (3)(-1)}}{2(3)} = 1/3 \pm \frac{\sqrt{16}}{6} = 1/3 \pm 2/3
					</me>.
					So <m>x = 1</m> or <m>x = -1/3</m>. For each such <m>x</m>, setting <m>y = 3/2 - x</m> gives us a critical point for <m>f</m>, so <m>f</m> has two critical points, i.e., <m>(1,1/2)</m> and <m>(-1/3,11/6)</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					To apply the second derivative test to each critical point, we calculate the discriminant of <m>f</m>. We find that
					<me>
						f_{xx} = 6x \quad f_{yy} = 2 \quad \text{and}\quad f_{xy} = 2
					</me>.
					So the discriminant is the function
					<me>
						D(x,y) = f_{xx}(x,y) f_{yy}(x,y) - f_{xy}(x,y)^2 = (6x)(2) - (2)^2 = 12x - 4
					</me>.
					So
					<me>
						D(1,1/2) = 12(1) - 4 = 8 \quad\text{and}\quad D(-1/3,11/6) = 12(-1/3) - 4 = -8
					</me>.
					Since <m>D(1,1/2)</m> is positive, and <m>f_{xx}(1,1/2) = 6</m> is positive, the critical point <m>(1,1/2)</m> is a local minimum of the function <m>f</m>. Since <m>D(-1/3,11/6)</m> is negative, this tells us that <m>(-1/3,11/6)</m> is a saddle point of the function <m>f</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
		<remark>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The following activity explains why we only look at <m>f_{xx}</m> in the first two cases of the second derivative test.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</remark>
		<activity>
			<statement>
				<p>
					True or False: If <m>D &gt; 0</m>, then <m>f_{xx}</m> and <m>f_{yy}</m> have the same sign.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					True. If <m>D = f_{xx}f_{yy} - (f_{xy})^2 &gt; 0</m>, then <m>f_{xx}f_{yy} &gt; (f_{xy})^2 \geq 0</m>, so <m>f_{xx} f_{yy} &gt; 0</m>. Since <m>f_{xx} f_{yy}</m> is positive, this implies either both <m>f_{xx}</m> and <m>f_{yy}</m> are positive, or both <m>f_{xx}</m> and <m>f_{yy}</m> are negative. So <m>f_{xx}</m> and <m>f_{yy}</m> always have the same sign when the discriminant is positive.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
	</subsection>
	<subsection xml:id="sec-second-derivative-test-and-saddle-points-3">
		<title>Contour plots and saddle points</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>The Hessian tests classify local behavior algebraically. Contour plots and restrictions to lines show the same behavior geometrically.</p>
		</introduction>
		<note>
			<p>
				A common way to detect a saddle point is to find two directions through the point such that the function increases in one direction and decreases in another. This is not a complete definition of every possible saddle point; it is a useful test in many examples.
			</p>
		</note>
		<p>
			For example, the function
			<me>
				f(x,y)=x^2-y^2
			</me>
			has a saddle point at <m>(0,0)</m>. Along the line <m>y=0</m>,
			<me>
				f(x,0)=x^2,
			</me>
			which has a local minimum at <m>x=0</m>. Along the line <m>x=0</m>,
			<me>
				f(0,y)=-y^2,
			</me>
			which has a local maximum at <m>y=0</m>. The contour plot below shows the same behavior.
		</p>
		<p>
			In this example, the surface increases in one coordinate direction and decreases in the other. This is the behavior suggested by the saddle shape.
		</p>
		<figure xml:id="fig-10-1-saddle-point-exampe">
			<caption>A contour plot of <m>f(x,y) = x^2 - y^2</m>. Adapted from: Stanford's MATH 51 textbook.</caption>
			<image source="figures/unit5/u5-saddle-contours-x2-minus-y2.png" width="70%">
				<shortdescription>
					Contour plot of x squared minus y squared with a saddle point at the origin.
				</shortdescription>
				<description>
					<p>
						The contour plot shows level curves of <m>f(x,y)=x^2-y^2</m>. The zero contour consists of two diagonal lines crossing at the origin. Positive and negative contour values alternate across the four regions, indicating a saddle point at <m>(0,0)</m>.
					</p>
				</description>
			</image>
		</figure>
		<remark>
			<statement>
				<p>
					More formally, if the lines are given in parametric form by
					<me>
						\{\mathbf{a}+t\mathbf{d}_1\}
						\qquad\text{and}\qquad
						\{\mathbf{a}+t\mathbf{d}_2\},
					</me>
					then the one-variable restrictions
					<me>
						g(t)=f(\mathbf{a}+t\mathbf{d}_1)
						\qquad\text{and}\qquad
						h(t)=f(\mathbf{a}+t\mathbf{d}_2)
					</me>
					can show opposite local behavior at <m>t=0</m>. In the example above, this verifies that nearby points occur on both sides of <m>f(\mathbf{a})</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</remark>
		<figure>
			<caption>At a saddle point for <m>f(x,y)=x^2-y^2</m>, there is a local minimum along <m>y=0</m> and a local maximum along <m>x=0</m>.</caption>
			<image source="figures/unit5/u5-saddle-surface-slices.png" width="80%">
				<shortdescription>
					Saddle surface showing an upward x-axis slice and a downward y-axis slice.
				</shortdescription>
				<description>
					<p>
						A translucent saddle surface for <m>z=x^2-y^2</m> is shown. The red curve along <m>y=0</m> opens upward and has a local minimum at the origin, while the blue curve along <m>x=0</m> opens downward and has a local maximum at the origin.
					</p>
				</description>
			</image>
		</figure>
		<activity>
			<statement>
				<p>
					For each point in the figure below, indicate whether it is a local maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point.
				</p>
				<figure>
					<caption>A contour plot with a variety of types of critical points.</caption>
					<image source="figures/unit5/u5-critical-points-contours.png" width="70%">
						<shortdescription>
							Contour plot with labeled local extrema and saddle points.
						</shortdescription>
						<description>
							<p>
								The contour plot shows level curves of the illustrative function <m>g(x,y)=2-(x^2-1)^2-(y^2-1)^2</m>. Point <m>P</m> is at the center of nested low-value contours, point <m>S</m> is at the center of nested high-value contours, and points <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m> are placed where the contour pattern changes like a saddle.
							</p>
						</description>
					</image>
				</figure>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					If we look at the collection of nested ovals centered on <m>S</m>, and inspect the numerical values labeling the level sets, we see those numbers are strictly increasing. These ovals seem to be honing in on a point <m>S</m> that is a local maximum. Likewise, the collection of nested ovals near <m>P</m> have function values going in a decreasing direction, suggesting <m>P</m> is a local minimum. We also see saddle points at <m>R</m> and <m>Q</m>. As we move away from <m>R</m> in the north-east direction, the function value increases, whereas moving away in the south-east direction causes the function value to decrease. Near saddle points, the contour plot forms an <q>X</q> shape dividing the space into <m>4</m> regions. Function values increase in one pair of opposite regions and decrease in the other pair. Near local extrema, level sets form oval shapes resembling ellipses.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
	</subsection>
</section>
